From 3fc025a75a1e968b8e879ec228c1abd4f4538aa5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 09:23:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(front): read every subscript of [][Next]_v TLC's
 collector reads, as it reads it

TLC's SubscriptCollector binds no LET definition, looks every name up
as an operator, and reads what an INSTANCE substitutes as a LazyValue
of the substitution. The front end's collector gave up, and declined
where TLC may warn, on shapes TLC reads plainly, so specs that matched
on main, where no subscript warning was printed, declined:
- a LET holding a function definition, which the collector couldn't
  bind; TLC reads a function definition's body, a function constructor,
  as a builtin it ignores;
- a LET holding an INSTANCE, whose copies the collector didn't look up
  in the LET, as reading the specification now does; what such an
  INSTANCE substitutes is TLC's LazyValue, a literal included, not a
  LET definition it holds as a value;
- a definition the configuration substitutes, of a constant or of a
  definition, with or without parameters, or by a builtin or a
  function: TLC holds the substitute's own node, whose body it enters,
  a literal too, or for a builtin its Java method's value, which it
  ignores.

TLC fails at the second next-state relation of a specification, after
that one's warnings and before any later conjunct, with message 2240;
the front end read every conjunct and failed with a message of its
own. It now stops and fails as TLC does.
